├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ beam_search_local_refinement                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the single random greedy construction with a deterministic beam search followed   │
│                          │ by makespan-based insertion local search.                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. The previous implementation evaluates only randomly sampled next transactions and      │
│                          │ performs a single construction, so it can easily discard the transaction whose placement  │
│                          │ avoids a costly read/write conflict later in the schedule.                                │
│                          │ 2. Beam search keeps several low-cost partial schedules simultaneously. At every depth it │
│                          │ evaluates every feasible next transaction for each retained prefix using                  │
│                          │ `get_opt_seq_cost`, which directly optimizes the real simulator makespan rather than      │
│                          │ proxies such as transaction length or write count.                                        │
│                          │ 3. The beam width is controlled by the existing `num_seqs` argument, so the caller’s      │
│                          │ search budget is now meaningful. Duplicate prefixes are removed and a deterministic       │
│                          │ lexical tie-break makes results reproducible.                                             │
│                          │ 4. The best completed beam schedule is further optimized with first-improvement           │
│                          │ relocation moves. Moving one transaction from any position to any other position can      │
│                          │ substantially reduce delayed read/write conflicts that a strictly forward greedy decision │
│                          │ cannot correct.                                                                           │
│                          │ 5. A small exact enumeration path is used for very small workloads, guaranteeing the      │
│                          │ optimal ordering when the factorial search is inexpensive.                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This directly evaluates conflict-induced makespan at every decision and repairs globally  │
│                          │ harmful early ordering choices, producing substantially better schedules than randomized  │
│                          │ sampled greedy selection.                                                                 │
