                        Patch Metadata - Gen 10/200 - Novelty: 1/3 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/3                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ diverse_multistart_local_search                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ The existing beam search keeps only prefixes with the smallest immediate makespan. This   │
│                          │ can prematurely discard schedules whose early conflict cost is slightly higher but which  │
│                          │ avoid a much longer read/write dependency chain later. The edit improves this in three    │
│                          │ stages:                                                                                   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. It increases the beam width while retaining an elite set plus diverse prefixes ending  │
│                          │ in different transactions. The last scheduled transaction strongly affects which data     │
│                          │ conflicts are exposed next, so this avoids beam collapse into near-identical orders.      │
│                          │ 2. It applies simulator-scored local optimization to several final beam schedules rather  │
│                          │ than only the single best beam result. Each seed is improved with both insertion moves    │
│                          │ and non-adjacent swaps. Insertions are effective for moving a transaction around a        │
│                          │ conflict chain, while swaps can cross local minima requiring two opposing moves.          │
│                          │ 3. It uses deterministic segment-reversal perturbations followed by the same exact local  │
│                          │ search. These temporary uphill moves let the search leave local minima without            │
│                          │ introducing random performance variation.                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Every decision continues to use `workload.get_opt_seq_cost`, so optimization is based     │
│                          │ directly on actual transactional conflict makespan rather than metadata-based heuristics. │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0367                                                                                   │
│ error_attempt            │ None                                                                                      │
