│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ multistart_vnd_schedule_search                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ Replace the single sampled greedy construction with a multi-start cost-aware search       │
│                          │ followed by variable-neighborhood descent.                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. The previous implementation ignored `num_seqs` and evaluated only a random subset of   │
│                          │ ten candidate transactions at each construction step. The replacement uses `num_seqs`     │
│                          │ independent starts and evaluates every remaining transaction for normal-sized workloads,  │
│                          │ choosing the transaction that gives the lowest actual prefix makespan.                    │
│                          │ 2. Multiple starting transactions are important because conflict direction is             │
│                          │ order-sensitive: a transaction that is poor as a first choice can be very beneficial      │
│                          │ after another conflicting writer has run.                                                 │
│                          │ 3. Each constructed schedule is evaluated with the simulator’s exact `get_opt_seq_cost`,  │
│                          │ and only the strongest constructed schedules are refined.                                 │
│                          │ 4. The local search uses three exact-cost neighborhoods: relocation, swap, and contiguous │
│                          │ two-transaction block relocation. Single moves repair local ordering errors, swaps handle │
│                          │ pair inversions, and block moves allow mutually beneficial transactions to move together  │
│                          │ when neither can improve independently.                                                   │
│                          │ 5. Every accepted change is a strict makespan improvement, so validity is preserved       │
│                          │ because every candidate is always a permutation of the original transaction identifiers,  │
│                          │ and the reported schedule cannot be worse than its initial greedy seed.                   │
